  That game started as a throw-away Wizard after my Arch ascension.
(That was the Arch I started in 2004).  It had the most incredible early
luck that just kept piling up.  I had to stop playing a number of times,
literally stunned by the Niagara Falls of good luck hitting me.  I'd
watch marvin while talking about the luck on #nethack.

  At the same time marvin (as mrivan) was playing a game with the most
miserable luck.  I'd be on #nethack talking about my latest good news
while Marvin was talking about working around his latest problem.  We
even talked some about his game.  He took all the problems right in
stride.  I was floundering around trying to think what to do next.

  I commented on his "complete lack of any normal human emotion" when
his bag of holding blew up.  I hope he wasn't offended.  I was pretty
off balance from my own game.  (_Popular Delusions and the Madness of
Crowds_, first published in 1841, says the people who got rich from the
South Sea Bubble went crazier than the people who went broke.  Believe
it.)

   Marvin was still on his streak of consecutive ascensions.  The RNG
had the LuckVac on Full Suck on his game and was emptying the bag into
mine.  (See the name of my luckstone.)  Marvin did ascend, but I think
it was his next game where he "died from playing while asleep." 

  Masque's pudding farm bones were found quite high in the dungeon.
He'd died from eating a cockatrice egg just before waking Rodney.
Grayswandir, HoB, Excalibur, BoH are just the barest high-lights.


  A quick check showed rings of increase damage and protection were
edible.  Edible rings of increase damage were the big factors in both
eit_brad's and zadir's record setting games.  Zadir had #quit game after
game until he got one with edible =oID.  My game was the game of a
lifetime.  

  I'd sometimes thought about going for high score, but realized it
would be even more tedious than pudding farming.  Well, I'd never have a
better chance.  Besides, this was just a throw away game.

  Folks, I'm here to tell you to never go for high score.  The only
thought worse than going on was the thought of quitting and wasting all
that work.  I didn't finish that game to get the high score.  That game
as finished to get it over with.


  I read eit_brad's post.  I read zadir's post, and re-read them, and
kept referencing them.  Then made copies and cross-referenced.  Read
spoilers and thought a lot.  It was a hell of a lot of mental effort.
Did lots and lots and lots of careful tests.  Without someone else's
trail to follow I couldn't have done it.

  Even worse was the stress of avoiding failure.  The thought of
premature DYWYPI haunted every keystroke.  The more effort I put in, the
worse the pressure got.

  Tracking my score and thinking about where to stop were some of the
things I did to take my mind off of the game. 

  Babamus, at #3, had broken 2*10E9 points first in early 2004.  Art, at
#2 had achieved Hp:21800000.  They'd both doubled their scores by
ascending.  Zadir had actually scored 2,147,483,647 points manually,
while dying from overexertion.


  Zadir, art and babamus didn't deserve to be beaten, but #3 was the
lowest position I'd accept.  I also wanted to leave room at the top for
the next martyr to obstinacy to make #3.

